_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,47,2075,15087,387,279,8381,2326,47311,448,198,59,7265,90,6612,9,532,47,7,16,8,14581,1124,839,220,16,11,90155,47,7,17,8,14581,1124,839,220,17,11,90155,47,7,18,8,14581,1124,839,220,18,11,90155,47,7,19,8,14581,1124,839,220,19,624,59,408,90,6612,9,92,12209,400,47,7,20,15087,646,387,13302,304,279,1352,400,32,1124,839,1124,37018,90,33,15170,34,2137,3,1380,400,32,4779,400,33,4779,323,400,34,3,525,6785,25780,11,323,400,34,3,374,10250,13,220,7379,400,32,488,425,488,356,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $P(x)$ be the degree three polynomial with\n\\begin{align*}\nP(1) &= \\log 1, \\\\\nP(2) &= \\log 2, \\\\\nP(3) &= \\log 3, \\\\\nP(4) &= \\log 4.\n\\end{align*}Then $P(5)$ can be expressed in the form $A \\log \\frac{B}{C},$ where $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are positive integers, and $C$ is prime.  Find $A + B + C.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"881d4085036bc81db82fbcace4d9384cedd2dcfb687302ae48f914d39756361e"},"seconds":76.76001954078674,"seed":916712680489813114,"stop":"eos","text":"Given $P(x)$ is a degree three polynomial satisfying the following conditions:\n\\[\nP(1) = \\log 1, \\quad P(2) = \\log 2, \\quad P(3) = \\log 3, \\quad P(4) = \\log 4,\n\\]\nwe aim to find $P(5)$ in the form \\(A \\log \\frac{B}{C}\\) with $A, B, C$ positive integers and $C$ prime, and then determine \\(A + B + C\\).\n\n1. **Construct the polynomial \\(P(x)\\):**\n\n   Since \\(P(x)\\) is a cubic polynomial, it can be written as:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Formulate the Lagrange Interpolating Polynomial:**\n\n   We can use Lagrange interpolation to find \\(P(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   P(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^4 \\log(i) \\cdot L_i(x),\n   \\]\n   where \\(L_i(x)\\) are the Lagrange basis polynomials given by:\n   \\[\n   L_i(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{1 \\leq j \\leq 4 \\\\ j \\neq i}} \\frac{x - j}{i - j}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate each \\(L_i(x)\\):**\n   \\[\n   L_1(x) = \\frac{(x-2)(x-3)(x-4)}{(1-2)(1-3)(1-4)} = -\\frac{(x-2)(x-3)(x-4)}{6},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_2(x) = \\frac{(x-1)(x-3)(x-4)}{(2-1)(2-3)(2-4)} = \\frac{(x-1)(x-3)(x-4)}{2},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_3(x) = \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x-4)}{(3-1)(3-2)(3-4)} = -\\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x-4)}{2},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_4(x) = \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)}{(4-1)(4-2)(4-3)} = \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)}{6}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the basis polynomials:**\n   \\[\n   P(x) = -\\log(1) \\cdot \\frac{(x-2)(x-3)(x-4)}{6} + \\log(2) \\cdot \\frac{(x-1)(x-3)(x-4)}{2} - \\log(3) \\cdot \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x-4)}{2} + \\log(4) \\cdot \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)}{6}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify \\(P(5)\\):**\n   \\[\n   P(5) = -\\log(1) \\cdot \\frac{(5-2)(5-3)(5-4)}{6} + \\log(2) \\cdot \\frac{(5-1)(5-3)(5-4)}{2} - \\log(3) \\cdot \\frac{(5-1)(5-2)(5-4)}{2} + \\log(4) \\cdot \\frac{(5-1)(5-2)(5-3)}{6}.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = -\\log(1) \\cdot \\frac{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}{6} + \\log(2) \\cdot \\frac{4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}{2} - \\log(3) \\cdot \\frac{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1}{2} + \\log(4) \\cdot \\frac{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2}{6}.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = 0 + 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 4 \\log 4.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 4 \\log 4.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 4 \\log (2^2).\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = 4 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3 + 8 \\log 2.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = 12 \\log 2 - 6 \\log 3.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5) = 6 (\\log 2^2 - \\log 3) = 6 \\log \\frac{4}{3}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Identify \\(A, B, C\\):**\n   - \\(A = 6\\)\n   - \\(B = 4\\)\n   - \\(C = 3\\)\n\n7. **Sum \\(A + B + C\\):**\n   \\[\n   A + B + C = 6 + 4 + 3 = 13.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
